The fppapi/v* header sets are FPP's own plugin headers — Copyright the Falcon
Player Developers, LGPL v2.1.  They are NOT vendored: this repo is unlicensed
and distributes no FPP code.  `make compat` extracts them into gitignored
directories from a local FPP checkout (FPP_SRC, default ~/projects/fpp) on
every run.  Only stub/ is hand-written, standing in for the third-party
libraries FPP itself pulls in (jsoncpp, libhttpserver, drogon).

  v80      8.0                src/Plugin.h
  v8       8.5.1              src/Plugin.h
  v9       9.5.3              src/Plugin.h
  v10      origin/v10.0-beta  src/{Plugin.h, fpphttp.h, fpphttp_types.h,
                                   fpp-json-fwd.h}
  vmaster  origin/master      src/{the v10 set + log.h}

Why the shapes matter: 8.x and 9.x declare `registerApis(httpserver::webserver*)`
against real libhttpserver (9.5.3's Plugin.h differs from 8.5.1's only by an
unrelated constructor and settingChanged).  FPP 10.0 adds a no-argument
`registerApis()` and demotes the old signature to a [[deprecated]] shim that
warns per route; the two v10 builds in ../Makefile cover the native path and
the shim fallback.  The libhttpserver-era libraries also differ in the
top-level cv-qualification of render_GET's return type — a hard error on
mismatch — so the plugin derives it with decltype, and stub/httpserver.hpp is
compiled BOTH ways (PM_STUB_CONST_RETURN) to prove the derivation.

vmaster carries log.h because master's Plugin.h includes it; stub/fpp-pch.h
defers to a real log.h when present, so that build exercises the real
LogInfo/LogErr expansions and the trantor LOG_* collision that
stub/drogon/HttpAppFramework.h reproduces on purpose.

`playlistInserted` — the callback the whole §13.1 mirror rests on — was added
to FPP on 2024-12-07 and first shipped in 8.5.  It is absent from 8.0 and 7.0,
which pluginInfo.json still declares support for; v8 is pinned at 8.5.1
because that is the OLDEST released line carrying it, and it is one of the
two rig boxes.  v10 is pinned at the v10.0-beta branch because that is the
line the plugin is currently tested against (10.x beta 2).
